even as the wards were being counted  ahead of these preliminary results many  believe that the outcome was  predetermined  one of tunisia's most prominent  opposition figures najib shebi claims  the process of this controversial  referendum was rigged  to secure the result kai saeed dissolved  the election committee and installed his  own we questioned this committee's  independence the results announced  contradict those noted by local and  foreign observers these results are fake  and overinflated 75 percent of tunisians  refused to go to vote on kai saeed's  constitution  despite the historical low turnout of 30  percent president kassaid was quick to  claim victory  he remains optimistic that this is the  beginning of a new chapter for the  country  it will be a real democracy and not a  formality as we've had for decades the  people will recover their wealth and  whoever committed crimes against the  tunisian people must be tried  but international reaction has been  cautious  we also knew concerns that the new  constitution includes weakened checks  and balances  that could compromise the protection of  human rights and fundamental freedoms  with the results now announced tunisia  will shift from a parliamentarian system  to an executive presidential one critics  say the president will now have full  control over parliament the government  as well as the judiciary in effect this  will put an end to the separation of  power in the country tunisia was the  spark for the arab spring that spread  across the region despite its ups and  downs for the last decade it has been  regarded as the only democracy to emerge  from the upheaval but now with a new  constitution coming into effect  questions are being raised are the arab  spring goals betrayed is this the end of  the arab spring the opposition still  believes that democracy will eventually  prevail  we have no choice but democracy now it's  in a relapse but it will win we see the  algerian movement hasn't ended in  victory iraq is not doing so well but  they will eventually triumph democracy  is now engraved in the arab mindset it  will not fade look at sudan algeria  lebanon i see a future for democracy i  see a setback in countries like egypt  and syria and other nations but i also  see it making a comeback  while a silent majority has chosen to  stay away from the polling stations  chaby's optimism about the future of  democracy remains not proven  rasul sardar al-jazeera tunis
 

